<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!-- A hospital staff member who actually WALKS: skeletal gait, arms and legs,
     not a rigid mesh sliding along the floor.

     This is an SDF <actor>, and that is the whole point. Gazebo only animates
     skinned meshes through actors; a <model> can be driven around but its mesh
     is rigid, so a "walking" model just skates. The skin and the walk animation
     are Luca's NurseFemaleWalk from Fuel, resolved out of the vendored
     fuel_cache like every other Fuel asset here.

     WHY THIS IS SAFE TO USE AS AN OBSTACLE, despite actors having no collision
     geometry. It was measured, not assumed: a gpu_lidar at the Husky's own scan
     height (0.447 m) was pointed at one of these actors standing 3.0 m away,
     and 2 of its 180 beams came back at 2.921 m. The LIDAR is a RENDERING
     sensor, so it sees the actor's skin exactly as it sees any other visual.
     That is what feeds /scan, the Nav2 costmaps and therefore the avoidance
     behaviour - so as far as navigation is concerned this is a real obstacle.

     THE LIMITATION, stated plainly: there is still no contact physics. If the
     robot ever did reach one, it would pass through rather than bump. That is
     acceptable here because the entire objective is never to touch them and the
     LIDAR is what makes that possible - but it does mean a collision cannot be
     detected by contact, and "no collision" has to be judged from separation
     distance instead.

     MOTION IS SCRIPTED, not commanded over a topic. The previous velocity-driven
     model pedestrians had to be steered by a node, and two of them jammed
     against walls and simply stopped - so "the pedestrians move" was not
     reliably true. A trajectory cannot jam: Gazebo interpolates the actor along
     its waypoints regardless of what else is in the world, every actor moves
     every run, and the walk cycle is driven from the same script so the gait
     matches the travel.

     Waypoints below are placeholders around the origin; every instance in a
     world overrides them - see aws_hospital.sdf, where each walker gets a
     stretch of corridor measured clear beforehand. -->
<sdf version="1.9">
  <actor name="hospital_walker">
    <skin>
      <filename>https://fuel.gazebosim.org/1.0/luca/models/nursefemalewalk/1/files/meshes/NurseFemaleWalk.dae</filename>
      <scale>1.0</scale>
    </skin>
    <animation name="walk">
      <filename>https://fuel.gazebosim.org/1.0/luca/models/nursefemalewalk/1/files/meshes/NurseFemaleWalk.dae</filename>
      <scale>1.0</scale>
      <interpolate_x>true</interpolate_x>
    </animation>
    <script>
      <loop>true</loop>
      <auto_start>true</auto_start>
      <delay_start>0.0</delay_start>
      <trajectory id="0" type="walk">
        <waypoint><time>0</time><pose>0 0 0 0 0 1.5708</pose></waypoint>
        <waypoint><time>10</time><pose>0 8 0 0 0 1.5708</pose></waypoint>
        <waypoint><time>12</time><pose>0 8 0 0 0 -1.5708</pose></waypoint>
        <waypoint><time>22</time><pose>0 0 0 0 0 -1.5708</pose></waypoint>
        <waypoint><time>24</time><pose>0 0 0 0 0 1.5708</pose></waypoint>
      </trajectory>
    </script>
  </actor>
</sdf>
